  "query_preview": "..(Monday through Saturday)\nIssued at 457 PM EDT SAT AUG 22 2026\n\nThe long-term period opens with quiet weather Monday morning. The\n22/12z model suite analysis shows mean troughing in place across \nthe eastern CONUS around a parent ~558 dam low spinning near/over\nthe Ottawa Valley. A smaller upstream closed ~570 dam low is \ntransiting from southern Alberta into Saskatchewan. Meanwhile, an \n~596 dam upper level high is centered near/over New Mexico. \nTranslating down to the surface, an ~1005 mb surface low is nearly\nvertically stacked under the parent low between James Bay and \nLake Ontario. Its cold front sweeps south-southeastward along the \nCarolina coast and then arcs southwest to west through the Gulf \nCoast states before extending back northwestward into the central \nPlains. Cool Canadian high pressure, cradled in the arc of the \ncold front, extends from northwestern Ontario to over the \nCommonwealth. Farther upstream, an ~1005 mb surface low is passing\nover southern Saskatchewan and dragging a trailing cold front \nthrough the Northern Rockies and Great Basin.\n\nFor Monday through Tuesday, heights will gradually rise over the\nOhio Valley as the first upper-level trough and its parent low\nshift away to the northeast and surface high pressure settles\nsoutheast, cresting over eastern Kentucky with PWATs close to 0.75\ninches. With time, a warm front will try to ease back northward\ntoward the Commonwealth, allowing PWATs to recover back toward 1.5\ninches by late Wednesday. However, heights will fall slightly again\nmid- to late week as the aforementioned closed upper low over the\nCanadian Prairies digs southeast, opens, and reinforces troughing\nover the eastern CONUS for the remainder of the work week. This \nwill support the next area of low pressure tracking into eastern \nCanada while its trailing cold front slides southward into the \nOhio Valley Thursday into Friday. Drier weather may follow a \nsecond cold front heading into next weekend.\n\nIn sensible weather terms, look for fair and cool weather through\nTuesday night, with lows ranging in the 50s to near 60F and highs\nin the lower to mid 80s. Fog is likely in the mainstem river\nvalleys late each night through mid-morning. Low to medium\nprecipitation chances in the form of diurnally modulated showers\nand thunderstorms return on Wednesday and persist through Friday\nas the first cold front works through the region, before trending\ndrier by Saturday. Look for highs ranging mainly in the mid 80s\nfrom Wednesday onward, while nighttime lows moderate into the 60s.",
